            <ArticleTitle>Spontaneous spinal epidural haematoma mimicking acute coronary syndrome.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>925-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.3978/j.issn.2223-4292.2015.01.07</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Spontaneous spinal epidural hematoma (SSEH) is an acute neurological emergency which carries significant morbidity unless diagnosed and treated in a timely fashion. Some cases of SSEH are idiopathic but there is a well-recognised association with deranged coagulation and abnormalities of clotting. In recent years there has been increasing availability of novel anti-platelet agents, often prescribed in the setting of suspected acute coronary syndromes (ACS) and following percutaneous coronary interventions and these agents also present an increased risk of SSEH. We present a case of SSEH following an acute presentation with chest pain and treatment with dual anti-platelet therapy.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
